From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] pipewire: size the output cushion in quanta, and stop one late callback blanking the stream

Measured from the codec's monitor ports on the Raspberry Pi appliance - the
samples the DAC actually receives, rather than a counter:

    7.81s of audio, 69 dropouts, EVERY ONE exactly 64 samples

One graph quantum each, ~8.8 per second. The device was clean throughout (ALSA
xruns zero) and the emulator was running well above realtime, so neither end was
at fault: single cycles where the producer had nothing ready and the callback
handed the graph a buffer of silence.

Two causes, both here.

The cushion was "one quantum plus one producer update" - 96 frames at a 64-frame
quantum, 2.2ms - which absorbs no jitter at all. It is now a configurable number
of quanta (MAME_PIPEWIRE_PREBUFFER_QUANTA, default 4). At q64 that is 5.8ms of
output cushion, still an instrument latency, unlike the 23ms that a 1024-frame
quantum costs to paper over the same jitter.

And a single short callback re-armed prebuffering, which replaces the following
callbacks with memset(0) until the cushion refills. abuffer::get() already
handles a shortfall gently - it repeats the last sample, explicitly "to prevent
audible pop" - so re-arming on top of that turned one frame of lateness into
whole buffers of digital silence, which is why every gap measured was an exact
multiple of the buffer size. The cushion is now rebuilt only after
MAME_PIPEWIRE_REARM_AFTER consecutive short callbacks (default 8).

Also in this patch, both found while chasing the above:

  * The pacing loop was unbounded, exiting only when the ring had drained to the
    cushion, so emulation progress was conditional on PipeWire continuing to
    consume. When consumption stalled the emulator stalled: at a 256-frame
    quantum the machine froze outright, LCD export stale, CPU at a few percent,
    systemd still reporting the service active, and the MPC stuck on its boot
    screen with nothing logging an error. MAME_PIPEWIRE_PACE_BUDGET_US bounds it.

  * m_prebuffer_frames and m_producer_frames are running maxima that only grow,
    and restarting pacing cleared the buffers but not those, so one oversized
    write inflated the cushion for the rest of the session.

  * MAME_PIPEWIRE_LATE_US makes the late-update threshold settable; hardcoded at
    5500us it showed only the worst outliers.

Note for whoever reads pw-top next: its client ERR column is not a dropout
counter. docs/system-placement.md already records that the emulator's nodes
accrued 1,600/s of it while the audio was provably clean, and this patch's
author spent an evening re-learning it.
---
 src/osd/modules/sound/pipewire_sound.cpp | 

diff --git a/src/osd/modules/sound/pipewire_sound.cpp b/src/osd/modules/sound/pipewire_sound.cpp
--- a/src/osd/modules/sound/pipewire_sound.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/modules/sound/pipewire_sound.cpp
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 
 #include <chrono>
 #include <cstdlib>
+#include <ctime>
 #include <cstring>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <map>
@@ -114,6 +115,7 @@
 		uint32_t m_updates_since_report;
 		uint32_t m_prebuffer_frames;
 		uint32_t m_producer_frames;
+		uint32_t m_consecutive_underruns;
 		uint32_t m_last_underrun_requested;
 		uint32_t m_last_underrun_available;
 		int64_t m_last_underrun_callback_gap_us;
@@ -122,7 +124,7 @@
 		std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point m_last_callback;
 		std::vector<int16_t> m_capture;
 
-		stream_info(sound_pipewire *wire, bool is_output, uint32_t osdid, uint32_t channels, uint32_t rate, std::string name = {}) : m_wire(wire), m_is_output(is_output), m_wait_stream(true), m_osdid(osdid), m_channels(channels), m_rate(rate), m_name(std::move(name)), m_stream(nullptr), m_buffer(channels, rate), m_last_underruns(0), m_last_overruns(0), m_observed_underruns(0), m_observed_overruns(0), m_updates_since_report(0), m_prebuffer_frames(0), m_producer_frames(0), m_last_underrun_requested(0), m_last_underrun_available(0), m_last_underrun_callback_gap_us(0), m_prebuffering(true), m_buffer_stats_ready(false) {}
+		stream_info(sound_pipewire *wire, bool is_output, uint32_t osdid, uint32_t channels, uint32_t rate, std::string name = {}) : m_wire(wire), m_is_output(is_output), m_wait_stream(true), m_osdid(osdid), m_channels(channels), m_rate(rate), m_name(std::move(name)), m_stream(nullptr), m_buffer(channels, rate), m_last_underruns(0), m_last_overruns(0), m_observed_underruns(0), m_observed_overruns(0), m_updates_since_report(0), m_prebuffer_frames(0), m_producer_frames(0), m_consecutive_underruns(0), m_last_underrun_requested(0), m_last_underrun_available(0), m_last_underrun_callback_gap_us(0), m_prebuffering(true), m_buffer_stats_ready(false) {}
 	};
 
 	static const pw_core_events     core_events;
@@ -138,6 +140,17 @@
 	std::map<uint32_t, stream_info> m_streams;
 	osd_common_t *m_osd = nullptr;
 	bool m_realtime_pacing = false;
+	// How long one main_update may spend waiting for the graph to drain.
+	// MAME_PIPEWIRE_PACE_BUDGET_US overrides it. The default is deliberately
+	// generous - it is a safety bound against a stalled consumer, not a tuning
+	// knob - and 33ms is two frames at 60 Hz.
+	uint32_t m_pace_budget_us = 33000;
+	// Report main-update gaps longer than this. MAME_PIPEWIRE_LATE_US overrides.
+	uint32_t m_late_report_us = 5500;
+	// Consecutive short callbacks before the cushion is rebuilt from silence.
+	uint32_t m_rearm_after = 8;
+	// Output cushion depth, in graph quanta. MAME_PIPEWIRE_PREBUFFER_QUANTA.
+	uint32_t m_prebuffer_quanta = 4;
 	bool m_audio_clock = false;
 	bool m_report_stats = false;
 	std::string m_capture_path;
@@ -516,6 +529,30 @@
 	m_osd = &downcast<osd_common_t &>(osd);
 	m_audio_clock = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_AUDIO_CLOCK") != nullptr;
 	m_report_stats = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_STATS") != nullptr;
+	if (const char *const budget = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_PACE_BUDGET_US"))
+	{
+		const long parsed = std::strtol(budget, nullptr, 10);
+		if (parsed > 0)
+			m_pace_budget_us = uint32_t(parsed);
+	}
+	if (const char *const quanta = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_PREBUFFER_QUANTA"))
+	{
+		const long parsed = std::strtol(quanta, nullptr, 10);
+		if (parsed > 0)
+			m_prebuffer_quanta = uint32_t(parsed);
+	}
+	if (const char *const rearm = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_REARM_AFTER"))
+	{
+		const long parsed = std::strtol(rearm, nullptr, 10);
+		if (parsed > 0)
+			m_rearm_after = uint32_t(parsed);
+	}
+	if (const char *const late = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_LATE_US"))
+	{
+		const long parsed = std::strtol(late, nullptr, 10);
+		if (parsed > 0)
+			m_late_report_us = uint32_t(parsed);
+	}
 	if (const char *const capture_path = std::getenv("MAME_PIPEWIRE_CAPTURE_WAV"))
 		m_capture_path = capture_path;
 	spa_zero(m_core_listener);
@@ -682,11 +719,25 @@
 
 	spa_buffer *sbuf = buffer->buffer;
 	int16_t *const output = (int16_t *)(sbuf->datas[0].data);
-	// Keep one graph quantum plus one producer update ready.  The extra update
-	// prevents a single late producer tick from immediately starving PipeWire.
+	// Keep a few graph quanta ready, not one quantum plus one producer update.
+	//
+	// "One quantum plus one update" is 96 frames at a 64-frame quantum - 2.2ms -
+	// which absorbs no jitter at all. Captured from the codec's monitor ports on
+	// the Raspberry Pi appliance, which is what the DAC actually receives:
+	//
+	//     7.81s of audio, 69 dropouts, EVERY ONE exactly 64 samples
+	//
+	// One quantum each, ~8.8 per second: single cycles where the producer had
+	// nothing ready and the callback handed over a buffer of silence. The device
+	// itself was clean throughout (ALSA xruns zero), so this is the whole defect.
+	//
+	// MAME_PIPEWIRE_PREBUFFER_QUANTA sets the depth. Four quanta at 64 frames is
+	// 5.8ms of output cushion - still an instrument latency, unlike the 23ms a
+	// 1024-frame quantum costs to paper over the same jitter.
 	stream->m_prebuffer_frames = std::max(
 			stream->m_prebuffer_frames,
-			uint32_t(buffer->requested) + stream->m_producer_frames);
+			std::max(uint32_t(buffer->requested) * m_prebuffer_quanta,
+					uint32_t(buffer->requested) + stream->m_producer_frames));
 	const auto callback_now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
 	const auto callback_gap_us = stream->m_last_callback.time_since_epoch().count()
 		? std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(callback_now - stream->m_last_callback).count()
@@ -707,7 +758,29 @@
 			stream->m_last_underrun_requested = buffer->requested;
 			stream->m_last_underrun_available = available;
 			stream->m_last_underrun_callback_gap_us = callback_gap_us;
-			stream->m_prebuffering = true;
+			// Do NOT re-arm prebuffering for a single short callback.
+			//
+			// abuffer::get() already handles a shortfall the gentle way: it
+			// repeats the last sample, explicitly "to prevent audible pop".
+			// Re-arming prebuffering on top of that replaces the next several
+			// callbacks with memset(0) - whole buffers of digital silence -
+			// until the cushion refills. So one frame of lateness became
+			// milliseconds of nothing, and every measured gap on the appliance
+			// was an exact multiple of the buffer size for that reason.
+			//
+			// Rebuild the cushion only when the stream is genuinely starved,
+			// not when it is merely a little late. MAME_PIPEWIRE_REARM_AFTER
+			// sets how many consecutive short callbacks that takes.
+			stream->m_consecutive_underruns++;
+			if (stream->m_consecutive_underruns >= m_rearm_after)
+			{
+				stream->m_prebuffering = true;
+				stream->m_consecutive_underruns = 0;
+			}
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			stream->m_consecutive_underruns = 0;
 		}
 	}
 	if (!stream->m_wire->m_capture_path.empty() && (stream->m_name == ":speaker"))
@@ -1017,6 +1090,14 @@
 				if (!stream.m_is_output)
 					continue;
 				stream.m_buffer.clear();
+				// Reset the cushion maxima too. m_prebuffer_frames and
+				// m_producer_frames only ever grow, so a single oversized write
+				// - a warp boot, a stall, a resize - inflates the cushion for
+				// the rest of the session and the latency never comes back
+				// down. Clearing the buffer while keeping its high-water marks
+				// meant a restart of pacing did not actually restart anything.
+				stream.m_prebuffer_frames = 0;
+				stream.m_producer_frames = 0;
 				stream.m_prebuffering = true;
 				stream.m_buffer_stats_ready = false;
 				stream.m_observed_underruns = 0;
@@ -1028,7 +1109,11 @@
 	else if (m_realtime_pacing)
 	{
 		const auto gap_us = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(update_now - m_last_main_update).count();
-		if (m_report_stats && (gap_us > 5500))
+		// The threshold is settable so the TAIL can be characterised rather than
+		// just noticed. 5500us only ever showed the worst outliers, which is
+		// enough to know something is wrong and not enough to know its shape;
+		// set MAME_PIPEWIRE_LATE_US=1000 to collect a distribution.
+		if (m_report_stats && (gap_us > m_late_report_us))
 		{
 			uint32_t queued = 0;
 			auto primary = m_streams.find(m_primary_output_id);
@@ -1044,6 +1129,23 @@
 		m_main_update_count++;
 	}
 
+	// Pace against the audio clock, but NEVER let the consumer stall emulation.
+	//
+	// This loop used to exit on one condition only: the ring having drained to
+	// the cushion. That makes the emulation thread's progress conditional on
+	// PipeWire continuing to consume, and when consumption stops the emulator
+	// stops with it - the machine freezes mid-frame, the LCD export goes stale,
+	// CPU falls to a few percent, and systemd still reports the service active.
+	// On the Raspberry Pi appliance that is exactly what happened at a 256-frame
+	// quantum: a frozen MPC sitting on its boot screen with nothing logging an
+	// error. Partially applied, it is also why the emulator was measured
+	// generating audio at 6% of realtime while pacing was engaged.
+	//
+	// The wait is now bounded. Pacing still holds the emulator back while the
+	// graph is healthy; when the graph stops draining, emulation proceeds and
+	// the audio buffer overruns instead - a recoverable, reported condition
+	// rather than a hung instrument.
+	const auto pace_deadline = update_now + std::chrono::microseconds(m_pace_budget_us);
 	while (m_realtime_pacing)
 	{
 		auto const primary = m_streams.find(m_primary_output_id);
@@ -1051,13 +1153,48 @@
 				(primary->second.m_buffer.available() <= primary->second.m_prebuffer_frames))
 			break;
 
-		// One PipeWire stream is the audio clock. Waiting for every auxiliary
-		// MPC output lets a delayed secondary stream stall the audible primary.
-		// A short timeout also prevents a missed callback signal from stalling
-		// the emulation thread indefinitely.
-		timespec deadline;
-		pw_thread_loop_get_time(m_loop, &deadline, 250'000);
-		pw_thread_loop_timed_wait_full(m_loop, &deadline);
+		if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() >= pace_deadline)
+		{
+			if (m_report_stats)
+				osd_printf_warning(
+					"PipeWire: pacing budget exhausted (%u us); proceeding with %u frames queued\n",
+					m_pace_budget_us,
+					primary->second.m_buffer.available());
+			break;
+		}
+
+		// SLEEP for the time the consumer needs to drain the excess, rather
+		// than waiting on the condition variable.
+		//
+		// pw_thread_loop_timed_wait_full() does not sleep on the Raspberry Pi
+		// appliance: the emulation thread spun here at 100% of its core while
+		// producing 5.9% of realtime audio - full speed by the CPU meter,
+		// almost nothing by the audio clock. The same code sleeps correctly on
+		// the development host, so this does not depend on the condvar being
+		// signalled the way one platform happens to signal it.
+		//
+		// The audio clock already tells us exactly how long to wait: frames
+		// queued beyond the cushion, divided by the sample rate, is the time
+		// the consumer needs to catch up. Sleeping that long paces the
+		// emulator to realtime while it runs unthrottled, which is the whole
+		// point of "full speed, sleep by the audio clock".
+		const uint32_t queued = primary->second.m_buffer.available();
+		const uint32_t cushion = primary->second.m_prebuffer_frames;
+		const uint32_t excess = (queued > cushion) ? (queued - cushion) : 1;
+		uint64_t sleep_ns = (uint64_t(excess) * 1'000'000'000ull) / primary->second.m_rate;
+		// Never sleep past the budget, and never busy-spin on a tiny excess.
+		const uint64_t budget_ns = uint64_t(m_pace_budget_us) * 1000ull;
+		if (sleep_ns > budget_ns)
+			sleep_ns = budget_ns;
+		if (sleep_ns < 50'000ull)
+			sleep_ns = 50'000ull;
+
+		pw_thread_loop_unlock(m_loop);
+		timespec req;
+		req.tv_sec = time_t(sleep_ns / 1'000'000'000ull);
+		req.tv_nsec = long(sleep_ns % 1'000'000'000ull);
+		nanosleep(&req, nullptr);
+		pw_thread_loop_lock(m_loop);
 	}
 	pw_thread_loop_unlock(m_loop);
 }
